---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const title = "Privacy — Dayseam";
const description =
  "How Dayseam handles your data: the desktop app (local-first, Keychain, no telemetry) and this marketing site (optional analytics with consent).";
---

<Base {title} {description}>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-16 pb-24">
    <header class="border-b border-white/10 pb-8">
      <p class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-strand-teal">Privacy</p>
      <h1 class="mt-3 text-balance text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-zinc-50 sm:text-4xl">
        Privacy &amp; data practices
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-pretty text-base leading-relaxed text-zinc-400">
        This page is the public summary for <strong class="text-zinc-300">App Store Connect</strong>, privacy
        questionnaires, and anyone evaluating Dayseam before install. Two surfaces: the
        <strong class="text-zinc-300">Dayseam desktop app</strong> (the product) and
        <strong class="text-zinc-300">this website</strong> (marketing only).
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-zinc-500">
        A longer engineering appendix (exact file paths, IPC boundaries, sandbox entitlements, and the update trust
        model) is maintained alongside releases for <strong class="text-zinc-400">security review, enterprise
        procurement, and App Store audit</strong>. It is not published on the public web; request it through the
        <strong class="text-zinc-400">support or security contact shown with the product</strong> (for example the Mac
        App Store listing once the app is live).
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="mt-12 space-y-12 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-zinc-300">
      <section aria-labelledby="desktop-heading">
        <h2 id="desktop-heading" class="text-xl font-semibold text-zinc-50">Dayseam desktop app</h2>
        <p class="mt-4">
          Dayseam is <strong class="text-zinc-200">local-first</strong>. Your reports, cached activity, and evidence
          live in a <strong class="text-zinc-200">SQLite database</strong> under your macOS user (Application Support).
          The legacy <strong class="text-zinc-200">direct-download</strong> build
          (<code class="text-zinc-300">dev.dayseam.desktop</code>) and the
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">Mac App Store</strong> build
          (<code class="text-zinc-300">dev.dayseam.mas</code>) use
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">different bundle IDs</strong>, so they keep
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">separate</strong> database folders if both are installed — remove the old copy
          from Applications when you switch.
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-zinc-400">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-zinc-300">Secrets</strong> (personal access tokens, API tokens, OAuth refresh tokens)
            are stored in the <strong class="text-zinc-200">macOS Keychain</strong>, not in plaintext in the database.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-zinc-300">Network</strong>: the app contacts
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">only</strong> the services you configure (e.g. GitLab, GitHub, Atlassian,
            Microsoft identity endpoints). There is
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">no Dayseam-operated cloud backend</strong> and no mandatory account.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-zinc-300">Telemetry</strong>: the app does <strong class="text-zinc-200">not</strong> ship
            analytics or crash-reporting SDKs. Outbound traffic is driven by your connectors only.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-zinc-300">Mac App Store</strong>: the public build is distributed through the App Store;
            updates come through Apple’s standard mechanism. Sandboxing and entitlements follow Apple’s requirements for
            the store build.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <div
          class="mt-6 rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-charcoal-900/40 p-5"
          aria-labelledby="legacy-dmg-heading"
        >
          <h3 id="legacy-dmg-heading" class="text-base font-semibold text-zinc-100">
            Legacy direct-DMG build: “Couldn’t check for updates”
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-zinc-400">
            If you installed Dayseam from a direct DMG before May 2026 and the app now shows
            <strong class="text-zinc-300"
              >“Couldn’t check for updates: Could not fetch a valid release JSON from the remote”</strong
            >, that is the expected end-state for the direct-download channel — it has been retired. The
            <strong class="text-zinc-300">Mac App Store</strong> is now the only way to keep receiving updates.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-zinc-400">
            The App Store build uses a different bundle id
            (<code class="text-zinc-300">dev.dayseam.mas</code> vs the legacy
            <code class="text-zinc-300">dev.dayseam.desktop</code>), so the two apps coexist without overwriting each
            other — your existing local data, tokens, and reports are not touched when you install the App Store build.
            Once you have moved over anything you want to keep, remove the old <code class="text-zinc-300">.app</code>
            from <code class="text-zinc-300">/Applications</code>.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-4">
            <a
              href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/dayseam/id6766103608?mt=12"
              class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm font-medium text-strand-teal hover:text-strand-teal/80"
              rel="noopener"
            >
              Install Dayseam from the Mac App Store
              <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
            </a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="website-heading">
        <h2 id="website-heading" class="text-xl font-semibold text-zinc-50">This website (dayseam.github.io)</h2>
        <p class="mt-4">
          The marketing site is <strong class="text-zinc-200">static</strong>. It does not run Dayseam and does not
          access your app data.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4">
          With your <strong class="text-zinc-200">consent</strong> (cookie banner), we may load
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">Google Analytics 4</strong> and
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">Microsoft Clarity</strong> to understand how visitors use these pages (e.g.
          page views, funnels, anonymised session quality). You can accept or decline; until you choose, those tags do
          not load. You can change your mind anytime via
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">Cookie preferences</strong> in the footer.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-zinc-400">
          That consent model applies <strong class="text-zinc-300">only to this site</strong>. It does not change the
          desktop app’s “no telemetry” posture described above.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="rights-heading">
        <h2 id="rights-heading" class="text-xl font-semibold text-zinc-50">Your choices &amp; contact</h2>
        <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-zinc-400">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-zinc-300">App data</strong>: delete the app and remove its Application Support folder if
            you want all local state gone; revoke tokens at each provider as you would for any API client.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-zinc-300">Security vulnerabilities</strong>: do not post details in public forums.
            Report them through the <strong class="text-zinc-300">security or support channel published with the
            product</strong> (Mac App Store “App Support” / vendor contact when listed, or the equivalent on your
            download channel). We treat coordinated disclosure seriously.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-zinc-300">General questions</strong>: use the <strong class="text-zinc-300"
              >support link on the Mac App Store page</strong> when the app is distributed there, or the contact options
            bundled with your download source. This site does not operate a separate helpdesk inbox.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-charcoal-900/40 p-6" aria-labelledby="updates-heading">
        <h2 id="updates-heading" class="text-lg font-semibold text-zinc-50">Keeping this page accurate</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-zinc-400">
          When the app’s behaviour changes in a way that matters for privacy (new data types, new network endpoints,
          new third parties), we update this page and the materials we share with reviewers in lockstep. Last reviewed:
          <strong class="text-zinc-300">May 2026</strong>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <p class="text-center text-sm text-zinc-500">
        <a
          href="/"
          class="text-strand-teal underline decoration-strand-teal/40 underline-offset-2 hover:text-strand-teal/90"
          >Back to home</a
        >
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
